Job submissions per project

run_estimation.sh commands per project, run from segmentation/ on the cluster.
They call sbatch for the project and activate the env (segamega,
PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1) for the jobs. The last column's command (--segment)
submits both steps at once: the segmentation job waits for the pose job to end
(sbatch --dependency=afterany) and then segments whatever pose files exist. To
resubmit only a segmentation job, use --segment-only with the --seg-* options.
run_estimation.sh picks each job's QOS from its time limit: normal up to 24
hours, medium up to 48 hours, long up to 7 days. With the commands below,
every job fits in normal.

Estimates are from ../time_estimation.md, fitted on the four projects rerun
with the current scripts. Pose estimation, with 8 videos at once on 8 CPUs,
takes about 26 s to start plus 1/140 s per frame (each video's process does
about 17.5 frames per second), so a job with fewer than 8 videos left waits
for its longest one. The estimate is per job: with --parts N, each of the N
array jobs. Segmentation takes about 4.3 s per file plus 2.5 ms per frame.
Frames = video length x FPS. Requested times are about 2x the estimate, with
a minimum of 1 hour. Jobs that run out of time can simply be resubmitted:
finished files are skipped. Estimates are in hours:minutes.


Pose estimation jobs have 8 CPUs and convert 8 videos at once, each with one
MediaPipe instance (--workers 1, so every instance tracks consecutive frames).
SMILE2 and SMILE are split over 10 and 2 array jobs (--parts), so they finish
in hours instead of days; each part gets --cpus, --mem and --time and writes
its own log, <project>_videos_to_poses_<part>.out. Segmentation gets 8 CPUs
for SMILE2 and SMILE (PyTorch uses all of them), 2 elsewhere.

Memory: in the four reruns, pose estimation peaked at 3.6-4.5G for all 8
processes together and segmentation at 1.0-1.6G (sacct MaxRSS), on clips of
up to about 700 frames. Each process holds the pose data of its video, which
grows with frames per file. So pose jobs get 8G, or 16-32G for projects with
long files (SMILE2, SMILE, SRT-2, SMILE_2018, Noah David: 35,000 to 100,000
frames per file), and segmentation of those projects gets 16-32G, as it
processes all of a file's 1024-frame chunks in one batch.

